      "math_academy_id": "e-3232",
      "problem": "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x - 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 4.$",
      "worked_solution": "We must have $x = 0$ at the $y$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (0-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ 9+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-1)^2 \\geq 0$ for all values of $y,$ there is no $y$ such that $(y-1)^2 = -5.$ Therefore, the circle has no $y$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14133180/assets/2c8fe8a8d7b0d8946fa5c7e1122df40d16175e0bc48353f53ac30487e3dea85e.png)",
